- Department: Students in technical or science departments (CSE, SWE, Pharmacy, EEE, Civil, Textile, MCT, CIS) are more likely to read academic books most of the time compared to students in departments like English, BBA, JMC, or others where academic reading might not be the main focus.
- Amount of time spent reading books per day: More hours spent reading correlates positively with reading academic books most of the time.
- Reason for reading books: If the reason is "Academic Purpose," this strongly suggests reading academic books most of the time; other reasons like entertainment or vocabulary improvement suggest less academic reading.
- How to manage books for reading: Students who use "Borrow," "Buy," or "Used-all" might be more engaged with academic books, while "E-books" or "Others" is less indicative.
- Number of e-books read last two years: Higher numbers (More than 20, 16-20) may indicate more academic reading if combined with other academic indicators.
- Number of printed books read last two years: Similarly, higher numbers suggest more academic reading.
- Favorite books for leisure time: If favorite books are "Non-Fiction" or "Biography," it might indicate a more academic inclination; "Fiction" or "Politics" may indicate less academic focus.
- What is the change felt after reading books: "Increase Academic Performance" or "Increase Problem Solving Skill" indicates academic reading; "Feel Relax" or "Increase Creative Thinking" may indicate non-academic reading.
- Who influenced you to read books: "Teachers" or "Self-Decision" might indicate academic motivation; "Friends" or "Parents" less so.
- Which language books do you like to read: Preference for "English" may correlate with academic reading, especially in technical fields.
- Where do you read printed books: Reading in "Library" or "Home" may indicate academic reading; "During Travel" may suggest leisure reading.
- Do you read Newspaper: Possibly unrelated or weak correlation.
- The format that you used for reading books: "Printed" or "Both" might correlate with academic reading; "E-books" alone less so.
- Frequency of reading books: "On everyday basis" strongly correlates with reading academic books most of the time; less frequent reading suggests otherwise.

